[Interview, 14 March 2024]

Researcher: Could you tell me a bit about how you got into nursing?

Participant: I came into it later than most people. I'd done about
seven years in retail management before I trained, and I think that
actually helped me a lot, because I knew how to deal with stressed
people. The first ward I worked on was respiratory medicine and it was
genuinely the hardest year of my life. I cried in the staff toilet
more than once. But I also remember the first time a patient I'd
written a discharge plan for came back to the ward weeks later just to
say thank you, and that's when I knew I wasn't going anywhere else.

Researcher: What kept you in it through the harder parts?

Participant: Honestly, two things. The team. I had a sister on that
ward who treated all of us new starters like we were her younger
siblings -- she was tough but she had your back. And the patients.
You'd have one really difficult shift and then a 90-year-old man would
hold your hand and tell you you reminded him of his daughter and the
whole thing would be worth it. I don't think I'd have stayed without
either of those.

Researcher: And how do you feel about the future?

Participant: I'm tired. I think most of us are tired. The pay isn't
great and we're stretched thin. But I'm not leaving. I genuinely
believe this is what I'm meant to be doing.



Analyse the transcript above for codes. Identify at all the codes from
the participant's responses, with at least 1 verbatim quote supporting
each code (preferably 2).




